{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Monophonic acid bass synth in the style of the Roland TB-303: a saw/square blend oscillator into a resonant 303-style ladder filter, the decaying filter envelope and the accent circuit that make the squelch, with slides between overlapping notes. Play it from MIDI (velocity 100 and above is an accent) or use the 16-step pattern sequencer: Key Sync runs the pattern while you hold a key (the key transposes it), Host Sync follows REAPER's transport and tempo. The Devil Fish knobs open up the envelope and slide times. MIDI CC 74 cutoff, 71 resonance, 81 env mod, 7 volume; pitch bend is +/- 12 semitones. Ported from Open303 by Robin Schmidt (rs-met.com) https://github.com/RobinSchmidt/Open303 (MIT) JSFX version released under GPLv3+ license Claude generated some of this code\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tape stop and tape start effect Ported from cStop 2.0.1 by Cal Goheen (TapeStop from cgo_modules, delay line and state variable filter from chowdsp_utils) https://github.com/calgoheen/cStop (GPLv3) JSFX code released under GPLv3+ license Claude generated some of this code\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Automatic pitch correction. The pitch of the input is measured and pulled to the nearest enabled note, chosen with the on-screen keyboard, a key/scale preset or live from MIDI notes. Bias, Filter and Correction set how hard and how fast it snaps, Offset and MIDI pitch bend shift the result, and Fast Correction trades a little accuracy for much lower latency. Each note can be detuned by up to a semitone for microtonal scales. Stereo is linked: pitch is measured on the mono sum and both channels are corrected together. Ported from fat1.lv2 by Robin Gareus, based on zita-at1 by Fons Adriaensen https://github.com/x42/fat1.lv2 (GPLv2+) JSFX version released under GPLv3+ license Claude generated some of this code\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A granular texture synthesizer in the style of Mutable Instruments Beads. It records the incoming audio into a buffer and replays it as a cloud of grains (seeded at random, regularly, by hand or by MIDI notes), or, with Size turned fully clockwise, as a multi-tap delay that turns into a beat slicer when frozen. Each knob has an attenuverter that adds random or smooth modulation, as on the module. Four quality settings change the buffer length and character, from clean 48 kHz digital to dirty 24 kHz mu-law tape with wow and flutter. Feedback, dry/wet and a reverb sit on the right. Switch the source to the wavetable synth for the module's built-in oscillator, playable from MIDI. Needs Dependencies/BeadTimeStory/beads_waves.txt for the wavetable synth. Ported from Beads by Emilie Gillet (Mutable Instruments) https://github.com/pichenettes/eurorack (MIT) JSFX version released under GPLv3+ license Claude generated some of this code\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Puts short beeps, filtered noise bursts or dips in level into any audio at a set interval, to protect demos, previews and stems from unauthorised use. Signal: Beep inserts a sine tone, Noise a low-passed noise burst (harder to remove cleanly), and Mute turns the material down instead of adding anything. Period sets the time between insertions, Duration how long each one lasts, and Freq the beep pitch or the noise filter's corner frequency. Gain sets the beep or noise level in dBFS. During the insertion the material is turned down in proportion, so the output never goes above the material's highest peak since playback started (when the beep is louder than that peak, the material is muted for its length). With Mute, Gain is the level the material drops to (-60 dB mutes it completely). Variation randomly changes the timing, duration, frequency and gain of every insertion, which makes automatic removal much harder. The pattern follows the project position, so every render is identical. Between insertions the audio passes through untouched, with zero latency, on any number of channels. Original design by chmaha, after the feature set of Voxengo Beeper JSFX code released under GPLv3 license Claude generated some of this code\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Little games to play when you need a breather, in two groups on the home screen: Zen and Games. Koi Pond: a calm pond with koi of eight varieties, lily pads, lotus flowers and falling blossom. Left-click to drop food and the koi come to eat; right-click or right-drag through the water to make ripples. Choose how many koi swim (1 to 12) and switch between day and night. There is nothing to win or lose. Patch Bay: a tangle of glowing, sagging patch cables. Drag the jacks around until no two cables cross. Level 1 starts with one crossing, level 2 with two, and so on, with more jacks as you go. Slingshot: press anywhere on the board, pull back and let go to launch a comet from the launch ring. The planets' gravity bends its path. Get inside the portal's swirling ring and it pulls you in to clear the level; grab the three stars on the way for a perfect shot. Hitting a planet, leaving the board or getting stuck in orbit costs you the shot. The aim guide and the portal's pull get smaller as you go, and later levels add pulsars that push away. Pixel Garden: a floating pixel-art island. Pick Flower, Tree or Mushroom and click a tile to plant a random variety; it grows from seed to bloom in real time and keeps growing while the window is closed. Decor places a bench, fountain, stone lantern, little pond, birdbath, rocks, a garden gnome or a bird feeder that robins, blue tits, goldfinches, cardinals and sparrows visit (and an owl at night). Water a plant to speed it up for a few minutes (rain showers now and then water everything), dig anything up with the shovel, press Grow all to see the finished garden, and switch the sky between day, sunset and night. Nothing ever wilts. Rain Window: rain running down a misted window, with an out-of-focus city at night, a city at dusk or a forest behind it. Big drops gather the small ones and slide down in fits and starts, leaving clear trails. Drag to wipe the mist (or draw in it); it slowly creeps back. Choose light, steady or heavy rain. Kintsugi: a broken piece of pottery waits on the table. Drag each shard to where it belongs; close is close enough, it settles into place and the crack fills with gold. Five glazes to mend, with more shards as you go. Firefly Shepherd: a night meadow full of fireflies that drift and flock together, each blinking in its own time. Every night has its own sky: from dusk to deep night, the moon waxes and wanes, shooting stars cross now and then, and dark nights may bring the northern lights or the Milky Way. Your light leads them; hold the mouse button to call them closer. Guide them into the paper lanterns, and when every lantern glows they float off into the sky. More lanterns each night. Signal Path: a grid of cable tiles, pedals and splitters between an audio input and a room full of speakers. Click a tile to turn it clockwise, right-click to turn it back. The signal lights up everything connected to the input; route it to every speaker with no loose ends to clear the level. Beat par for the layout for a perfect routing. The grid grows as you go. Peggy: aim the launcher and click to drop a ball into a field of pegs over a painted landscape. Pegs light up when the ball hits them and clear once it leaves the board. Clear every red peg before your ten balls run out; long bounce chains score more, gold pegs split the ball in two, and the catcher sliding along the bottom gives the ball back. Every red you clear heats the gauge on the right, multiplying your points by 2, 3, 5 and finally 10. The last red peg gets the slow-motion treatment, then golden rain: every peg left turns gold and is worth ten times as much for the rest of that shot, and the catcher gives way to five buckets worth 0, 2500, 5000, 10000 and a 25000 jackpot. After level 10 the boards keep changing: pegs that spin and slide, red pegs tucked behind others, teal bumpers that kick the ball away, a restless catcher, and a showpiece level built around one giant shape every five levels. Aim with the mouse, or with the left and right arrow keys (hold Shift for fine aim) and drop the ball with the down arrow or Enter. Mute Point: a mixing desk with a grid of glowing red mute buttons. Clicking a button flips it and the buttons directly above, below and beside it. Unmute every button to get all channels live; match par for a perfect mix. Hint lights up a button worth pressing. The desk grows from three channels to eight as you go. Bubble and Squeak: field mice are trapped in bubbles hanging from a branch on a moonlit night. Aim the launcher with the mouse and click to shoot; bubbles bounce off the side twigs. Three or more of a colour pop, and anything left hanging falls, so knocking loose a big cluster scores the most. Free every mouse before your shots run out: each one floats down on a dandelion seed to the mushroom house. Right-click or click the acorn to swap the next bubble in. Or use the keyboard: left and right arrows aim (hold Shift for fine aim), the up arrow shoots and the down arrow swaps. Popping fills the power jar, which loads a bomb, a rainbow bubble (matches any colour) or a fireball (burns straight through). Later levels add bomb and lightning bubbles in the branch, stones that never pop, and a branch that sinks a row every few shots. Krypton Gigablast: a vertical shooter through five alien worlds, two levels each. Steer through living walls and barriers (they block you and can crush you against the bottom of the screen, but never hurt on touch), swarms of creatures, collect the credits they drop and spend them at the shop halfway through and at the end of every level: a stronger main gun, side and rear shots, homing missiles, a laser, drones, energy, extra ships and smart bombs, or sell what you have for half the price. Every level ends with a guardian or a boss, and the Hive Mind waits at the end of world five. Mouse: steer, hold the left button to fire, right-click for a smart bomb. Keyboard: arrows steer, hold S to fire, D detonates a smart bomb. Each level, each return from the shop and each new ship starts with a 3-2-1 countdown before you take control. Flop House: no-limit Texas Hold'em against twelve rogues, from Lucky Lou in the back room to Mr Grim in the vault. Every table is a sit-and-go: blinds keep rising and the last player with chips wins it. Each character has a real playing style and a tell you can spot (a whistle, a wink, a sweat, a glowing antenna), and knocking one out adds it to your Rogues' gallery. Read tokens show how strong a hand really is, the optional Coach gives your win chance and pot odds, and Hands shows the rankings and your collection. All-ins get win percentages and a slow river. Cards are shuffled fairly and the opponents never see your hand. Mouse only. Hold Your Horses: the whole British racing year, Flat and National Hunt, on real courses with their real direction of running and typical circuit lengths. Career: buy horses at the monthly sales, train them, enter them in any race on the calendar (the 2:50 at Doncaster, the Derby, the Grand National) and pick jockey and tactics or ride yourself. Going and weather change every day, meetings can be abandoned, and over jumps horses sometimes fall. Or let the game pick a random day or a one-off race and bet with a fresh bank, or follow today's real fixtures from the 2026 and 2027 lists. Close finishes go to a photo. Mouse only. Hop to It: hop as far as you can across roads, railways and rivers without getting squashed. Ten worlds, each with its own hazard, and the seasons change as you travel. All ten worlds and eleven hoppers are open from the start, and 40 coins in a world unlock its bonus hopper. Stand still too long or fall behind and the eagle swoops. Two players can share a keyboard, split screen or battling on one screen. Arrow keys or W A S D hop, or click the way you want to go. Press P in any game to pause it (the music stops too) and P again, or Resume, to carry on. The pause panel also has the help card, the Sound button and Reset this game, which returns just that game to how it was the first time you opened it. The Games button goes back to the home screen. Your progress is saved with the project, and Start over takes you back to level 1. Koi Pond and Pixel Garden remember their settings, and the garden itself. Audio passes through untouched. Game sound is on by default and one Sound button, on the home screen and in each game, cycles All, FX only and Off for the whole plugin. It only plays while the transport is stopped or paused, so it never ends up in a render or recording. Original design by chmaha JSFX code released under GPLv3 license Claude was used in the creation of this product AI-assisted not vibe-coded Hold Your Horses course shapes (c) OpenStreetMap contributors, https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright. The course geometry data block in this file is made available under the Open Database License (ODbL). The music (all BreakRoom-*.opus files) comes from Pixabay under the Pixabay Content License and is not covered by the GPL; see the -LICENSE.txt files next to them.\par}
}

