\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Automatic double tracking for vocals and other sources: four pitch-shifted, delayed copies spread across the stereo field, with high- and low-pass filters and separate dry and wet levels, ported from ADT by Christopher Arndt https://github.com/SpotlightKid/adt (MIT) JSFX version released under MIT license Claude Opus 5.x may have generated some of the port of this code\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Chip-synth instrument with the sound hardware of classic game consoles. NES (2A03): two pulse channels, triangle, noise and DPCM drums, emulated with Nes_Snd_Emu by Shay Green, http://slack.net/~ant/libs/audio.html (LGPL-2.1+). Famicom expansion audio: Konami VRC6 (two pulse channels with eight duties and a sawtooth), emulated with Nes_Snd_Emu's Nes_Vrc6_Apu, or Konami VRC7 (six two-operator FM channels, an OPLL core with 15 built-in patches and one custom patch), emulated with ymfm's OPLL core by Aaron Giles (BSD-3-Clause) playing the VRC7 patch ROM read from the die by Nuke.YKT (as in emu2413 and the nesdev wiki). Game Boy (DMG): two pulse channels, wave channel and noise, emulated with MAME's Game Boy sound core by Wilbert Pol and Anthony Kruize, https://github.com/mamedev/mame (BSD-3-Clause). Master System / Game Gear (Sega PSG, SN76489): three tone channels and noise, emulated with MAME's SN76496 core by Nicola Salmoria and others (BSD-3-Clause). Mega Drive / Genesis (YM2612 or YM3438 FM plus the Sega PSG): six four-operator FM channels with an FM patch editor and sampled drums through the DAC, emulated with ymfm by Aaron Giles, https://github.com/aaronsgiles/ymfm (BSD-3-Clause). PC Engine / TurboGrafx-16 (HuC6280): six 32-step wavetable channels, noise and sampled DDA drums, emulated with MAME's c6280 core by Charles MacDonald (BSD-3-Clause). Super NES (S-DSP): eight BRR sample voices with Gaussian interpolation, hardware ADSR and the 8-tap FIR echo, emulated with snes_spc by Shay Green, http://slack.net/~ant/libs/audio.html (LGPL-2.1+), playing Doug Fraker's collection of 203 BRR samples (https://github.com/nesdoug/SNES_13, public domain per its author, repository MIT), with four drum kits; each MIDI channel picks its own sound. Unlike real hardware, all samples stay available at once. Layer mode plays every note on the same set of channels; the Chips setting stacks copies of the chip so chords can be played, and MIDI channel 10 plays a drum kit. Layer can also play one NES, Game Boy or Master System channel on its own (the triangle alone for bass, say), and the Game Boy and PC Engine have a custom wave you draw with the mouse, saved with the project. Tracker mode maps MIDI channels to the hardware channels of one chip, as game music drivers did. JSFX version released under GPLv3+ license Claude generated some of this code\par}
}

