{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A port of Mutable Instruments Warps, the meta-modulator: two signals go in and one comes out, combined through a continuously morphing set of algorithms. Algorithm sweeps from crossfade through wavefolding, analog and digital ring modulation, XOR and comparator to a 20-band vocoder with release and freeze. Timbre changes the character of the current algorithm, and in the vocoder it shifts the formants. Input 1 (carrier) comes from channels 1+2 and input 2 (modulator) from channels 3+4; the Input selector can use L/R instead. Send a second track into channels 3/4 for sidechained modulation. INT OSC replaces input 1 with the internal oscillator (sine, triangle or saw; saw, pulse or noise in the vocoder), and Level 1 then sets its pitch, or play it from MIDI. The Easter egg turns the module into a frequency shifter. Levels and timbres are the module's own, so very hot signals saturate as they would on the real thing. Needs Dependencies/WarpFactor/warps_tables.txt. Ported from Warps by Emilie Gillet (Mutable Instruments) https://github.com/pichenettes/eurorack (MIT) JSFX version released under GPLv3+ license Claude generated some of this code\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Makes any audio sound like it is playing from an old record. Drone: a slow, off-centre wobble in pitch that turns once per revolution of the record at the chosen Speed (33, 45 or 78 rpm, or anything in between). Aging: narrows the frequency range around the Frequency setting, the way old, worn records lose their lows and highs and gain a honky middle. Seven noise layers, each with its own on/off switch, level and pitch: Hum, Motor, Static, Noise, Rumble, Crackle and Crinkle. Bypass crossfades over 1024 samples, then passes the audio through untouched. Ported from Calf Vinyl by Markus Schmidt (Calf Studio Gear, https://calf-studio-gear.org), GNU LGPL 2.1. The noise layers play through a port of FluidSynth 2.6.1 (Peter Hanappe and others, GNU LGPL 2.1), exactly as Calf does. JSFX version released under GPLv3 license Claude generated some of this code\par}
}
